1. PHYSICAL FOUNDATIONS (TIER A)

1.1 Governing Equations

At the velocities and frequencies encountered (v ≤ 15 m s⁻¹, f ≤ 2 kHz), the displacement‑current term is negligible. The quasi‑static Maxwell equations apply exactly:

\nabla \times \mathbf{H} = \mathbf{J}

\nabla \times \mathbf{E} = -\frac{\partial \mathbf{B}}{\partial t}

\nabla \cdot \mathbf{B} = 0

\mathbf{J} = \sigma (\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B})

where

· \mathbf{H} = magnetic field intensity (A m⁻¹)
· \mathbf{J} = eddy‑current density (A m⁻²)
· \mathbf{E} = electric field (V m⁻¹)
· \mathbf{B} = magnetic flux density (T)
· \mathbf{v} = velocity of the conductive plate relative to the magnet array (m s⁻¹)
· \sigma = electrical conductivity of the plate (S m⁻¹)

1.2 Eddy‑Current Damping Principle

For a thin conductive plate moving with velocity v in the x-direction through a magnetic field having a normal component B_z, the motional electromotive force per unit length in the transverse direction is

\mathcal{E}_{\text{motional}} = (\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{y}} = v B_z .

The induced eddy‑current density is

\mathbf{J}_e \approx \sigma (\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}).

The Lorentz body force density \mathbf{f} = \mathbf{J}_e \times \mathbf{B} acts opposite to the motion (Lenz’s law). The total retarding force on the plate of volume V_{\text{plate}} is

\mathbf{F}_d = \iiint_{V_{\text{plate}}} (\mathbf{J}_e \times \mathbf{B}) \, dV .

In the linear regime, defined by the magnetic Reynolds number R_m \ll 1, the force is strictly proportional to velocity:

\mathbf{F}_d = -C_{\text{em}} \, \mathbf{v} \qquad \text{(Tier A for the functional form)}.

This velocity‑proportional behaviour is ideal for occupant protection: the damping force is self‑regulating (stronger in more severe crashes) and vanishes at zero velocity, eliminating stick‑slip and static friction.

1.3 Magnetic Reynolds Number Validation

The condition R_m \ll 1 must hold for linearity:

R_m = \mu_0 \sigma v L_{\text{char}},

with \mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} H m⁻¹, L_{\text{char}} = 0.05 m (one spatial period of the Halbach array). For copper (\sigma = 5.8 \times 10^7 S m⁻¹) and a worst‑case relative seat velocity v = 5 m s⁻¹:

R_m = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \times 5.8 \times 10^7 \times 5 \times 0.05
      \approx 0.018 \ll 1 .

The linear proportionality F_d = C_{\text{em}} v is therefore physically exact over the entire expected velocity range. (Tier A)

1.4 Skin Depth Regime

The electromagnetic skin depth at angular frequency \omega is

\delta = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\mu_0 \sigma \omega}} \qquad \text{(Tier A)}.

The characteristic frequency seen by the plate is \omega_{\text{eff}} = k v, where k = 2\pi/\lambda is the spatial wavenumber of the magnet array. For \lambda = 50 mm and v = 3 m s⁻¹:

k = \frac{2\pi}{0.05} = 125.7\ \text{rad m}^{-1}, \quad
\omega_{\text{eff}} = 125.7 \times 3 = 377\ \text{rad s}^{-1},

\delta_{\text{Cu}} = \sqrt{\frac{2}{4\pi \times 10^{-7} \times 5.8 \times 10^7 \times 377}}
                 \approx 8.5\ \text{mm}.

For a plate thickness d = 4 mm, we have d < \delta at velocities up to ≈11 m s⁻¹, justifying the thin‑plate approximation (uniform current through thickness) for the primary design window. (Tier A for the calculation; Tier B for the velocity estimate)

