2. HALBACH ARRAY FIELD MODEL

2.1 Ideal Infinite Array

A linear Halbach array with spatial period \lambda (pole pitch \tau = \lambda/2) and magnet height h_m produces a one‑sided intensified field. For an infinite planar array, the normal component of the flux density at distance g above the working face is

B_z(x,g) = B_r \left(1 - e^{-k h_m}\right) e^{-k g} \sin(k x) \qquad \text{(Tier A)},

where

· B_r = remanent flux density of the magnet material (T)
· k = 2\pi/\lambda = wavenumber
· g = air gap between magnet surface and conductive plate.

2.2 Finite Array Correction

For a finite array of N_p pole pairs, edge effects reduce the peak field. The fundamental amplitude is modulated by an aperture factor F_{\text{edge}} that depends on N_p and the observation position. For N_p \ge 4, F_{\text{edge}} \approx 0.85–0.90 in the central region, dropping to ≈0.75 near the edges. The corrected peak field is

B_0 = B_r \left(1 - e^{-k h_m}\right) e^{-k g} \, F_{\text{edge}} .

F_{\text{edge}} must be determined by FEA for a specific geometry. (Tier B)

2.3 Back‑Iron Enhancement

A soft‑iron back‑plate (relative permeability \mu_r \approx 1000–4000) behind the magnets channels flux that would otherwise escape, increasing the useful field by a factor \kappa_{\text{bi}}. For a back‑iron thickness h_{\text{bi}}:

\kappa_{\text{bi}} \approx 1 + (\mu_r - 1)\frac{h_{\text{bi}}}{h_{\text{bi}} + h_m}.

For h_{\text{bi}} = 5 mm and \mu_r \approx 4000 (M19 silicon steel), \kappa_{\text{bi}} \approx 1.2–1.4. Saturation limits the gain; FEA is required for exact values. (Tier B)

2.4 Dual‑Array Sandwich

The ESEMS employs two Halbach arrays facing each other with the conductive plate in between. To first order, the total damping force doubles:

F_d^{\text{total}} = 2 C_{\text{em}} v .

The effective field at the plate mid‑plane (gap g/2 from each array surface) is

B_{\text{eff}} = 2 B_0 e^{-k(g/2)} \left(1 - e^{-k h_m}\right) \approx 2 \times (\text{single‑array } B_0).

