4. OCCUPANT DYNAMICS & ENERGY ABSORPTION (TIER B/C)

4.1 Relative Seat Velocity During Crash

The key unknown is v_{\text{rel}}(t), the velocity of the seat plate relative to the vehicle floor. After lock release, the occupant and seat continue forward while the vehicle decelerates. In the first ~20 ms before seatbelt and airbag fully engage, the relative velocity can be bounded by the vehicle’s velocity change:

v_{\text{rel,max}} \approx a_{\text{veh}} \cdot \Delta t.

For a 30 g crash pulse over 20 ms, a_{\text{veh}} \approx 294 m s⁻², giving v_{\text{rel,max}} \approx 5.9 m s⁻¹. A more conservative 20 g pulse yields ~3.9 m s⁻¹. The central design point is v_{\text{rel}} = 2 m s⁻¹, with a plausible range of 1–5 m s⁻¹. (Tier B, requiring sled‑test validation)

4.2 Stroke Budget

Available forward displacement of the seat before occupant‑to‑interior contact is constrained by:

· Steering wheel proximity (driver): ~150 mm
· Seatbelt pretensioner stroke: ~15 mm
· Safety margin: ~30 mm

Allocable EM‑module stroke: s_{\text{max}} = 70–80 mm. This is geometrically feasible on the driver side; CAD verification for each vehicle platform is required.

4.3 Energy Absorption Estimate

The electromagnetic energy dissipated over the stroke is

E_{\text{abs}} = \int_0^{s_{\text{max}}} F_d\, dx = C_{\text{em}} \int_0^{s_{\text{max}}} v(x)\, dx .

Using an average velocity v_{\text{avg}} \approx 0.75 m s⁻¹ and the central C_{\text{em}}:

E_{\text{abs}} \approx 28\,200 \times 0.75 \times 0.075 \approx 1.58\ \text{kJ}.

Total occupant kinetic energy at 56 km h⁻¹ (15.56 m s⁻¹) for m = 80 kg:

E_{\text{kin}} = \tfrac{1}{2} \times 80 \times (15.56)^2 \approx 9.68\ \text{kJ}.

Fraction absorbed ≈ 1.58 / 9.68 ≈ 16 % (central estimate). With the C_{\text{em}} uncertainty band, the fraction ranges from ~7 % to ~30 %. (Tier B)

This energy absorption directly reduces the mechanical work that must be done by the seatbelt and airbag, and it reshapes the deceleration pulse by front‑loading the energy removal, which is favourable for HIC reduction.

