5. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE

5.1 Core Proposition

The ESEMS is a seat assembly incorporating a linear eddy‑current energy absorber that is mechanically locked during normal driving (zero drag) and released by a pre‑crash detection system before first contact. The resulting velocity‑proportional damping force reshapes the occupant deceleration without replacing any existing safety component. This architecture is novel relative to existing pyrotechnic or hydraulic active seat systems and to eddy‑current dampers used in suspension applications.

5.2 Module Layers

Layer 0 – Linear Guide Rail Assembly
Two precision ground rails (Thomson 440C stainless, dynamic load rating ≥ 8 kN each) with low‑friction carriages. Stroke: 80 mm. Operating temperature: −40°C to +120°C. Rail deflection under 5 kN lateral load estimated < 0.3 mm (FEA required). (Tier B)

Layer 1 – Lower Halbach Array (Fixed to vehicle floor)
4 pole pairs, λ = 50 mm. NdFeB N52 AH (high‑temperature grade, 150°C). Magnet size: 50×30×20 mm per segment. Back‑iron: 5 mm M19 silicon steel. Total mass incl. housing: ≈4.8 kg. Remains stationary relative to vehicle.

Layer 2 – Conductive Damping Plate (Moving with seat)
Oxygen‑free high‑conductivity copper C10100, dimensions 300 mm × 250 mm × 4 mm. Mass: ≈2.69 kg. Electrically isolated from the seat frame by a PTFE sheet. The plate is the sole moving electromagnetic component.

Layer 3 – Upper Halbach Array (Lock‑release mechanism)
Mirror image of Layer 1, mounted above the plate. In the locked state, it is rigidly coupled to the lower array, and the entire sandwich moves with the seat/vehicle body. Upon crash activation, the coupling between the arrays and the seat sub‑frame is released, fixing both arrays to the floor. The plate, now attached only to the moving seat, slides through the fixed magnetic gap.

Layer 4 – Adaptive Coil Bank (Optional)
Up to eight pancake coils (150 turns, 22 AWG) wound on the back‑iron faces allow modulation of the effective magnetic field by |\Delta B| \le 0.4 T. This enables real‑time variation of C_{\text{em}} for waveform shaping. Powered by an ultracapacitor bank.

Layer 5 – Seat Structure
Standard automotive seat frame, cushion, and harness anchors, mounted on the moving sub‑frame. No occupant‑interface modification.

5.3 Lock‑Release Mechanism

The lock is a normally‑coupled device: a permanent magnet or spring holds the coupling pins engaged, and a brief current pulse through a release coil neutralizes the magnetic latch. This polarity ensures that loss of electrical power leaves the seat locked (safe). Actuation time: ≤ 5 ms (solenoid/PM‑latch hybrid). A pyrotechnic shear pin provides backup activation (single‑use, < 1 ms).

Failure modes (see Section 9):

· Lock fails to release (false negative): ESEMS inactive, crash protection reverts to baseline airbag/seatbelt. No additional injury. (Tier A)
· Inadvertent release (false positive): Mitigated by CPI sustain requirement and mechanical end‑stops.
· Partial release: Dual latch points are mechanically coupled; single actuator signal releases both.

The false‑negative mode is the dominant design target, as it carries zero incremental risk.

