7. CONTROL LAW (ACTIVE MODE)

In the baseline passive configuration, C_{\text{em}} is constant; damping force is F_d = C_{\text{em}} v_{\text{rel}}. This yields a naturally front‑loaded deceleration pulse.

In the active configuration (coil bank installed), the ECU adjusts coil current at 1 kHz to approach a constant‑deceleration profile, which minimises the HIC:

a_{\text{occ}}^{\text{target}} = \frac{\Delta V_{\text{occ}}}{T_{\text{stroke}}} = \text{const}.

The required total force is F_{\text{target}} = m_{\text{occ}} a_{\text{occ}}^{\text{target}}. The seatbelt contributes F_{\text{belt}}(t) (estimated from pretensioner model or load cell). The electromagnetic force must supply:

F_{\text{EM,req}}(t) = F_{\text{target}} - F_{\text{belt}}(t).

From F_d = C_{\text{em}} v_{\text{rel}}, the required damping coefficient is

C_{\text{em,req}}(t) = \frac{F_{\text{EM,req}}(t)}{v_{\text{rel}}(t)}.

The coil current needed to achieve this is:

I_{\text{coil}}(t) = \frac{g_{\text{eff}}}{\mu_0 N_{\text{turns}}}
\left[ \sqrt{ \frac{C_{\text{em,req}}(t)}{\sigma d w l \, F_{\text{edge}}} } - B_0 \right],

with g_{\text{eff}} = 2g + h_m. The calculation is closed‑form and implementable in < 10 µs on a standard ECU. (Tier A for algebra; Tier B for achievability)

Limitations:

· As v_{\text{rel}} \to 0, C_{\text{em,req}} \to \infty; controller must saturate and hand over to the seatbelt below a threshold velocity (~0.3 m s⁻¹).
· Maximum coil current is physically bounded; the achievable C_{\text{em,max}} limits the force envelope. A Monte Carlo study over occupant mass and crash severity is required to quantify saturation probability. (Tier C)

