Appendix A: Derivation of the Eddy‑Current Damping Force

A.1 Governing Equations

At the velocities and frequencies of interest, the displacement current \partial \mathbf{D}/\partial t is negligible. The electromagnetic field in the conductive plate is then governed by the quasi‑static Maxwell equations:

\begin{aligned}
\nabla \times \mathbf{H} &= \mathbf{J} \quad &\text{(Ampère’s law)} \\
\nabla \times \mathbf{E} &= -\frac{\partial \mathbf{B}}{\partial t} \quad &\text{(Faraday’s law)} \\
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{B} &= 0 \quad &\text{(Gauss’s law for magnetism)} \\
\mathbf{J} &= \sigma (\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B}) \quad &\text{(Ohm’s law for moving media)} .
\end{aligned}

Here \mathbf{v} is the velocity of the conductor relative to the laboratory frame in which \mathbf{B} is static.

A.2 Induction in a Thin Plate

Consider a plate of thickness d (small compared with the skin depth and with the field variation scale), moving in the +x direction with speed v. The magnet array produces a flux density predominantly in the z‑direction: \mathbf{B} = B_z(x,y)\,\hat{\mathbf{z}}. The motional electric field seen by the plate is

\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B} = v\,\hat{\mathbf{x}} \times B_z\,\hat{\mathbf{z}} = -v B_z\,\hat{\mathbf{y}} .

Because the plate is thin, we can neglect the electric field build‑up in the y‑direction except for the eddy‑current loop closure, and we assume that the induced current density is predominantly in the y‑direction:

J_y \approx \sigma (v B_z) .

This is the “thin‑plate approximation” — the current is uniform through the thickness and completes its circuit at the plate edges.

A.3 Lorentz Force on the Plate

The Lorentz body force density acting on the currents is

\mathbf{f} = \mathbf{J} \times \mathbf{B} = J_y\,\hat{\mathbf{y}} \times B_z\,\hat{\mathbf{z}} = J_y B_z\,\hat{\mathbf{x}} .

Since J_y \approx \sigma v B_z,

\mathbf{f} \approx -\sigma v B_z^2\,\hat{\mathbf{x}} .

The total retarding force is the volume integral:

\mathbf{F}_d = \iiint_{V_{\text{plate}}} \mathbf{f}\, dV
           = -\hat{\mathbf{x}} \, \sigma d \iint_{A} v B_z^2 \, dA .

For a rigid plate moving at uniform instantaneous velocity, v is constant across the area, giving

\mathbf{F}_d = -\hat{\mathbf{x}} \, \sigma d v \iint_{A} B_z^2 \, dA .

The integral of B_z^2 over the plate area depends on the magnet geometry and plate size; in the simplest case of a uniform field B_0 over the entire plate area A = wl,

\iint B_z^2\,dA \approx wl B_0^2 .

Thus

F_d = \sigma d w l B_0^2 \, v = C_{\text{em}} v .

This is the fundamental linear viscous damping law.

A.4 Linearity Condition — Magnetic Reynolds Number

The neglect of the induced magnetic field (the “low R_m” approximation) is justified when the magnetic Reynolds number

R_m = \mu_0 \sigma v L_{\text{char}}

is much less than 1. The characteristic length is the spatial period of the field, L_{\text{char}} = \lambda = 2\pi/k. For copper and v \le 5\,\text{m s}^{-1},

R_m \approx 4\pi\!\times\!10^{-7} \times 5.8\!\times\!10^7 \times 5 \times 0.05 \approx 0.018 \ll 1 .

Therefore the induced field is negligible and the force‑velocity relation remains linear over the entire design envelope.

